(Ἀκερσεκόμης), a surname of Apollo expressive of his
      beautiful hair which was never cut or shorn. (Hom. Il. 20.39;
       Pind. P. 3.26.)

The fragments of Acusilaüs have been published by Sturtz, Gerae, 1787;
        2nd ed. Lips. 1824; and in the " Museum Criticum," i. p. 216, &c.
        Camb. 1826.

2. A surname of Circe, the sister of Aeetes. (Hom. Od. 9.32;
       Apollon. 4.559; Verg. A.
       3.386.) Her son Telegonus is likewise mentioned with this surname. (Acaeus, Propert. 2.23.42.)

The Varia Historia has been translated into Latin by C. Gesner, and
          into English by A. Fleming, Lond. 1576, and by Stanley, 1665;
         this last has been reprinted more than once.

Other personages of this name are mentioned in 

      Apollod. 3.15.8; Diod. 4.16;
       Eustath. ad Hom. p. 776; Hom. Od. 4.124. [ALCYONIDES.]

Freind's Hist. of Physic, whose words have been sometimes borrowed;
       Fabricius, Bibl. Graec. vol. xii. p. 593, sq. ed. vet.; Haller, Bibliotheca Medicinae Practicae, tom. i.; Sprengel, Hist.
        de la Méd. tom. ii.; Isensee, Geschichte der Medicin ;
       Choulant, Handbuch der Bücherkunde für die Aeltere
       Medicin.

(Ἀμπυκίδης), a patronymic from Ampycus or Ampyx, applied
      to Mopsus. (Ov. Met. 8.316, 350, 12.456, 524;
       Apollon. 1.1083; comp. Orph. Arg. 721.)

Anaxagoras explained his dualistic system in a work which is now lost, and we know it only
       from such fragments as are quoted from it by later writers, as Plato, Aristotle, Plutarch,
       Diogenes Laertius, Cicero, and others.

8. A Greek rhetorician, who taught at Nicomedeia in the reign of Domitian. (Eudoc. p. 58 ;
      Suid. s. v.
      Σιρίκος.)

(Ἀντήνωρ), a Greek writer of uncertain date, wrote a work
      upon the history of Crete, which on account of its excellence was called Δέλτα, inasmuch as, says Ptolemy Hephaestion (ap. Phot. Cod. 190, p. 151b. Bekk.), the Cretans called that which is good Δέλτον. (Aelian, Ael. NA 17.35;
      Plut. de Mal. Herod. 100.32.)

2. A daughter of Antiochus the Great, married to Ariarathes, king of Cappadocia, bore to her
      husband two daughters and a son named Mithridates. (Diod. xxxi. Ecl. 3;
      Appian, App. Syr. 5.)

(Ἀντιόχος). There are three
      mythical personages of this name, concerning whom nothing of any interest is related. (Diod. 4.37 ; Paus. 1.5.2, 10.10.1; Apollod. 2.4.5, &c.;
       Hyg. Fab. 170.)

(Ἀντίων), a son of Periphas and Astyageia, and husband of
      Perimela, by whom he became the father of Ixion. (Diod. 4.69;
      Schol. ad Pind. Pyth. 2.39.)

Plin. Nat. 7.52 ; Cic. de Fat. 3;
        V. Max. 1.8.16, ext.; Jacobs, Anthol.
