Cornelius was a man of blameless private life, and, in his public character, though he was
      accused of factiousness by the nobles, seems to have advocated usful measures. (Asconius, in Cic. pro Cornel. ;
      D. C. 36.21, 23; Drumann's Gesch. Roms, ii. p. 613.)

